when it comes to breast cancer which  affects nearly 4 million women black  women have the odds stacked against them  that's because they are 40 percent more  likely to die from the disease than any  other group the reasons are many denise  valdez explores why in our buddy check 8  report  [Music]  while black and white women get breast  cancer at about the same rates black  women are 40 percent more likely to die  from the disease and are also more  likely to be diagnosed before the age of  40 when annual screenings typically  start but it goes much deeper than that  dr joanna fulayan is an ob gyn in las  vegas she's confident there are at least  two main reasons for the disparity the  first black women's access to health  care lack of access to medicine meaning  they can't afford  medical care they don't have insurance  it's expensive to get a mammogram the  second and maybe even bigger obstacle a  mistrust of the medical profession to  let them know that  we  want to work with them  we are like them basically to teach them  that we are aware of what happened in  the past and we're going to make sure  that that does not happen again she's  referring to the documented experiments  on african americans in the days of  slavery these are things that occurred  but would never ever happen again in  this day and age black women tend to  receive a later diagnosis often with  more aggressive types of breast cancers  so annual screenings are vital but she  also urges her patients to know their  body i think that women should  definitely concentrate on  get doing their self breast exams  i think they should do it every month i  think they should do it a week after  their menses dr felian knows firsthand  the importance of early detection and  treatment she's a breast cancer survivor  herself  for buddy check denise valdez live local  now
 

